<?xml version='1.0' ?>
<rrx namespace="ApplicationObjects" v="9.1.10.3">
  <com ref="Datacap.Libraries.ApplicationObjects.Actions"  qi=" : Actions that create and manipulate DCO objects.">
    <h>
      Datacap applications all contain a hierarchy of Datacap Application Objects,
      which is more commonly called the application's DCO hierarchy.
      The DCO defines the structure of the objects of the application.  At the top level is the Batch object.  
      There is only one batch object. All
      objects are off of this main root node. The other objects are Document, Page and Field.  A typical structure
      would look something like this:<br/><br/>

      Batch<br/>
      - Document<br/>
      - - Page<br/>
      - - - Field<br/><br/>

      A Batch node can have a child Document, Page or Field. 
      Documents can also have child Documents, Pages and Fields.  Pages can only have child Pages, and Fields.
      Fields can only have child fields.<br/><br/>

      Page objects are usually a child of a Document and are named and arranged in a way that is
      meaningful for a specific application.  One exception is the page type "Other".  "Other" is a common
      page type in Datacap applications.  As documents are ingested to create a batch, they are assigned the
      initial type of "Other". This type is used as a starting point for processing documents.
      Unlike other page types, the Other page is directly attached to the Batch node.  Other application specific
      page types are typically attached to a Document object to group one or more pages or page types together
      in a meaningful way.<br/><br/>

      After files are ingested and assigned a type of Other, the next step is to identify and process the files.
      The "Other" pages are typically processed then assigned new page 
      types that define a more detailed hierarchy that groups
      pages together under a Document.<br/><br/>
      
      Each page type can have a unique set of fields that indicate
      areas on the page where data will be extracted.  A page typically defines the kind of page, such as
      an "Invoice", the fields define the type of data expected on a page such as Date, Vendor Name,
      Address, Invoice number, and other data expected on an Invoice.<br/><br/>
      
      It is also possible for a page to have different
      locations of data based on the document although the same page type is used.  For example, this is commonly
      performed in the APT application where invoices are processed, while each invoice has the same types of
      data such as names, addresses, dates, line items, the location of this data can be different based
      on the fingerprint of the page.  For example, in one invoice the date may be on the top left,
      another invoice may have the date in the top right, and still another 
      may place the date somewhere else on the page.
      This allows an application to process the same type of page
      consistently although the pages and location of data on each page is different.<br/><br/>

      While there is only one Batch node, there can be multiple nodes of all of the other types.
      An application specific hierarchy can consist of any combination of these objects that
      defines the documents, pages and fields required to process a set of files as defined
      by specific use cases.<br/><br/>

      Here is one example:<br/>
      For illustration (B=Batch, D=Document, P=Page, F=Field)<br/><br/>
      
      B MyApplication<br/>
      - P Other<br/>
      - D Invoice<br/>
      - - P MainPage<br/>
      - - - - F Date<br/>
      - - - - F Invoice Number<br/>
      - - - - F Invoice Amount<br/>
      - - - - F Line Items<br/>
      - - - - - F Quantity<br/>
      - - - - - F Description<br/>
      - - - - - F Price<br/>
      - - P TrailingPage<br/>
      - D Mortgage<br/>
      - - P Agreement<br/>
      - - - F Signature<br/>
      - - - F Date<br/><br/>

      While this is a very simple example, it shows a typical structure used in applications.  Each of these nodes is
      a DCO node and each are used to represent the current state of the application as well as 
      providing a way to attach processing rules and actions that will be acted upon each node within the
      application that process the data.<br/><br/>
      
      Each DCO node can have a "property" / "variable" that holds metadata about the node.  These are defined
      directly on the node.  For example, a page
      will have a variable that contains the name of the file associated with the page. There are some sets of variables
      that are standard however any number of additional user required variables can be created at any time.<br/><br/>

      A DCO node can contain any number of variables or children.  While objects typically have a specific type of child,
      there are some exceptions.  For example, a Page or a Field can be attached directly to a Batch node.  Likewise,
      a Field node can in turn have additional children fields, as is required for line item processing.<br/><br/>

      Each application has a "Setup DCO" and a "Runtime DCO".  A Setup DCO is the static hierarchy that defines the object layout
      of the application.  It is called the "Setup DCO" because it is defined at application setup / design time.  It
      defines one of all of the possible types of nodes that will be processed.  
      The setup DCO is also used to define the relationship of each application object
      to the application rules that will be run on each object.  For example rules can be attached to the "MainPage" object that will
      run on each object that has been identified as a MainPage.  The same rules or different 
      sets of rules can run on the TrailingPage.<br/><br/>

      The Runtime DCO is the dynamic version of the object hierarchy that directly reflects a specific batch that is being
      processed and contains all of the currently instantiated objects.  
      For example, if there are 10 pages in the batch, there will be 10 page objects existing in the Runtime DCO.
      The types of these pages may be mixed.  Perhaps there are 3 MainPages, 9 TrailingPages, all children of a set of 3 Invoice document objects
      and 1 Agreement page under a Mortgage document object.  The runtime DCO will contain the values of each of the variables
      contained in each object node.  At runtime, DCO nodes are created for the pages, documents and fields within the batch
      to reflect the unique files and data within the batch.<br/><br/>

      The primary focus of the Application Object action library is on manipulation of these DCO nodes.  Every action library
      will read, act on, and can also update an object.
      The Application Object library is more general for updating, organizing and deleting DCO nodes themselves.<br/><br/>

      Access of variables and nodes is case sensitive but it is not recommended to use names and types 
      for objects that differ only in case.<br/><br/>

      User variables can be configured in the setup DCO. Any object that has a variable preconfigured will automatically
      have that value unless it is changed at runtime.  For example, a DCO node may be configured with a
      variable value so all objects of that type, will initially have the value set in the setup DCO, then specific
      nodes can have that variable updated at runtime, as required by specific application use cases.<br/><br/><br/>
      

      <b>Predefined DCO Variables</b><br/><br/>

      The following is a list of common variables and attributes that are used to process an application:<br/><br/>
      
      <b>ID</b> : The unique identifier for the object.<br/>
      <b>TYPE</b> : The type of the object which corresponds to the setup DCO.<br/>
      <b>STATUS</b> : The status has many meanings and refers to the current state of the object.<br/>
      <b>IMAGEFILE</b> :  The file associated with a page node. Typically this is a TIFF, but it can be any type of file.<br/>
      <b>PageName</b> : Often used to hold the original file name without the extension.<br/>
      <b>ScanSrcPath</b> : The path and name of the source file.<br/>
      <b>DATAFILE</b> : The name of the runtime dco for the fields. This file must only be manipulated using actions.<br/>
      <b>TemplateID</b> : The ID of the fingerprint associated with the page.<br/>
      <b>CCOFILE</b> : The name of the CCO file associated with the page.<br/><br/>

      These common predefined variables can be read and written by any application.  
      While an application can freely read these variables, care should be taken to update these variable using provided actions.
      If a variable is updated incorrectly, it can cause problems at runtime.  If there is a need to update these variables,
      there are often predefined actions to update the variables.  For example, the TemplateID can be updated using the SetPageFingerprintID
      or the status can be updated using SetDCOStatus or SetDCOType to update the type.  The ID of a node is assigned when it is created
      and should not be changed once created. For example, a new child node can be added at runtime using AddChildDCONode and the
      ID for that child is specified as a parameter.  
      Once created, the ID of a node should not be changed or results can be unpredictable.<br/><br/><br/>
      

      <b>DCO Status</b><br/><br/>
      
      Each DCO has a status property.  The status indicates if the data associated with the node is valid, invalid
      or indicates a particular state or problem.  Verify panels are the primary place the status is used
      although actions may set the status or make logic decisions based on the status of an object.<br/><br/>      

      The status is updated at run time depending on the object type and the rules and actions of the task.
      Some actions may update the status of a DCO object.  It is also possible for a failed ruleset
      to automatically change the status of an object to fail, if the Status Preserve option is disabled.<br/><br/>

      Some common status values are shown in this table with their conventional meanings.  Commonly used
      status values are -1, 0, 1, 2, 49.  Other status status are less common and may or may not be used
      as listed depending on the configuration and uses cases for the application.<br/><br/>
      
      If you are using an uncommon value for status, it is recommended that the listed values are used
      instead of making up a new status value,
      if they apply to your application. If a status is required by an application that is not
      listed in the predefined values, a new unique value can be used in the 
      application, however it will be unique for your application and may be updated
      by an action that updates the status.  Actions can be used to look for the unique status
      and control the logic flow of rules based on that status value.<br/><br/>
            
      -1 Hidden / Ignore. The object should be ignored. If set on a field, the field is not displayed in the verify panel.<br/><br/>
      
      0 OK / Passed. An object with no known issue or has had a problem corrected so it is now valid.  This status may be used
      at any DCO level.<br/><br/>
      
      1 Error / Problem. A verify panel and actions can take specific actions for failed pages. This status may be used at any DCO level.<br/><br/>
      
      2 Overridden. Used for pages that fail validation but are overridden by the operator.<br/><br/>

      3 InvalidType. Set by document creation if a page type is invalid.<br/><br/>
      
      48 RecogDoneOK. Indicates a page that has been successfully processed by the Recognition task and is ready for Verification.
      This status does not mean that data in all fields on the page is complete and accurate.<br/><br/>

      49 ScanOK.  Scan tasks will typically uses this as an initial page status.<br/><br/>

      50 ScanBad. Indicates that unsuccessful scanning has resulted in a problem page.<br/><br/>

      51 CannotFindAnchor. Indicates that the workflow’s Recognition task has failed to identify the anchor fields of the page.      
      A batch containing a page with this status usually must be reviewed by a user in a "FixUp" panel.<br/><br/>

      52 DontNeedVerification. Verification can be skipped. Typically a status of 0 would be used instead.<br/><br/><br/>

      65 Indicates that although Recognition successfully identified the anchors on the page, a processing error prevented further steps.
      Pages with this status should be treated as problems.<br/><br/>

      66 Verification Done. Indicates that a page has been successfully processed by a "FixUp" task operating in the Verify mode.<br/><br/>
      
      70 RescanPage. Identifies the page as needing to be scanned again.<br/><br/>

      71 VerificationFailed<br/><br/>
      
      72 PageOnHold. Suspends the processing of a page, often when a page requires special attention, and places its document on Hold as well.
      If any page remains on Hold after the task processes the other pages in a batch, Taskmaster automatically assigns a Hold status to the batch itself.<br/><br/>
      
      73 PageOverridden<br/><br/>
      
      74 NoData. Ensures that none of the data on the page will be forwarded to an Export task – in effect, deleting the page.
      The assignment of this status to a page, usually by an operator or supervisor, results in a Pending status for the document.<br/><br/>
      
      75 DeletedPage. Marks a page and its parent document for deletion and removes their association with the sponsoring batch.
      This status does not, however, directly affect the corresponding Image file which can still exist in the batch.<br/><br/>
      
      76 Export completed. Indicates that the entire workflow has successfully processed a page.<br/><br/>

      77 DeleteApproved. Indicates that an operator or supervisor has approved the deletion of the page and document.<br/><br/>
      
      78 Removed. “Removes” the page from the workflow and its data from further consideration.      
      It also removes the document containing the page. In most cases, a page with this status is beyond repair.<br/><br/>
      
      79 ReviewPage.  Branches a page to a child job for review and possible repair.<br/><br/>

      128 DeletedDoc. Indicates that an authorized operator or Supervisor has removed a document from the internal organization of the batch.<br/><br/>

      129 Rescan. Indicates that the document needs to be scanned again.<br/><br/>

      145 ReviewDoc. Marks the document for review by a remedial FixUp task.<br/><br/>

      147 IncompleteDoc. Denotes a document with the wrong number or types of pages.<br/><br/><br/>
      

      <b>DCO Type and Page Identification</b><br/><br/>
      
      Images and files are ingested using the scan or virtual scan task.  During the ingestion, the pages are initially
      assigned the page type of "Other".  This is a special page type that does not have any document structure and
      uses the batch as a parent.  This is the starting point for processing of ingested files.<br/><br/>
      
      The next goal is to change the page type from "Other" to another meaningful page type.  
      A DCO type cannot contain double quotes. Once assigned
      a meaningful type, the pages can be processed and manipulated based on the use cases for
      the application. While page identification is 
      the next goal, it is not necessarily the next direct processing step.  The next step can vary from application
      to application.<br/><br/>
      
      For example, if images are ingested as PDFs, word documents, emails or other types of formats, one of the
      use cases may be to recognize these pages so the text within them can be analyzed, verified and stored.
      In this situation, before performing page ID, these kinds of documents are typically converted into TIFF
      images. The final result is that the original source file, such as a PDF, is still in the batch, but now
      there are individual images for each page from within the parent file.  Similar work is typically performed
      on emails or other types of documents that become a parent for multiple images, one new image
      for each page.<br/><br/>
      
      Another common step prior to page ID is to perform image enhancements on all or some of the images.
      If recognition is going to be performed and the image source isn't a nice clean document, such as
      a Word document, then the image may need some image adjustments.  While leaving out many
      details from this description, typically operations like deskew, automatic rotation, line removal
      and other enhancements are performed to create clean images to provide to recognition.
      The result would be a clean document that has proper orientation and is not skewed. 
      It is also sometimes useful to remove any lines from the document in this step.<br/><br/>
      
      The actual page ID process can be performed in a number of ways and varies based on
      specific use cases.  One approach would be to perform fingerprinting after image enhancement.
      Fingerprint matching directly assigns a type ID to the page, changing it from "Other" to
      the correct type. The fingerprinting actions will look at a page, and the known
      fingerprints and match the page to the correct fingerprint, so now the page type
      is determined along with the locations of any fields on the page.<br/><br/>
      
      Using barcodes or patch codes are another technique for determining the type of a page and
      then assigning the page type with the SetPageType action. This could be a barcode
      directly on the page to identify, or a separator page that is scanned before the current
      page that that indicates the page type of the following page.<br/><br/>
      
      Other types of identification can include performing recognition and identifying a page
      by specific text within the page that the application knows belongs to a specific type type
      and then directly assigning the page type.  If a page is blank, that may be assigned a different
      page type or possibly deleted or used as a separator.<br/><br/>
      
      Datacap provides a number of different kinds of actions that can be used to identify
      the page type and then assign it.  Again, what is appropriate depends on the
      application use cases.  Custom actions could even be used to use specific logic
      to figure out a page type and then assign it to the page.  A page could also be
      provided to a classification engine, which will determine the correct page 
      type and fingerprint.<br/><br/>
      
      Some applications use manual page identification with the Flex ID panel to have operators
      set page types by reviewing the document, 
      or may simply supplement manual identification only for a smaller set of pages that could not
      be automatically identified.  The actions are the tools to perform the
      identification process, how to arrange them to achieve the
      required page identification use cases is specific to an application.
      Most applications use some common
      techniques as described.<br/><br/>
      
      Once the Page ID rules have set new page types for each of the "Other" pages, then the "Create Documents"
      ruleset can be called which will automatically organize all of the pages into the appropriate 
      page hierarchy, grouping pages together under a document node, or multiple document nodes, as
      defined by the application.  Once page identification is done and the new document structure has
      been created, then processing of the batch can continue, but now that the page types are
      identified, then specific rules, and different rules, can be run on each object based on
      the DCO node level and the type.<br/><br/>
      
      After creating documents, the CreateFields action in the ApplicationObjects library will
      generate the field objects for a page. Initially these fields exist with no position
      information.  The position information is then determined by loading the zones
      for the associated fingerprint or using actions to dynamically find zone locations
      using rules at runtime.<br/><br/>

      Once all the pages are identified and documents created, subsequent actions that
      have been defined on the objects will run to perform the specific
      operations required for each type of object within the DCO hierarchy.<br/><br/><br/>

      <b>Field Confidence</b><br/><br/>
      
      A field represents a specific type of data from a field.  While it is not required,
      a field usually also has position information that identifies the location
      on the associated parent page object where the data exists.  This position information
      is a representation of the pixel area on the page from where the text
      was recognized in a format of: left, top, right, bottom.<br/><br/>
      
      Field objects have the ability to store recognized text that is associated
      with the field.  For example, if the field represents a date, the position would
      indicate the location of the text and the field's value would be the date, such as
      "01/01/2020".<br/><br/>

      Each character within the field's value also has an associated confidence level.
      The confidence level represents if the recognition engine thinks the value that
      was read is accurate or could have been misread. The confidence level is
      represented as a number between 1 to 10, where 1 means the engine is very unsure
      that the character is correct and 10 means the engine is very sure the number is
      correct.  Typically a confidence value of 8, 9 or 10 are considered "high" confidence
      and that the engine was probably correct in reading the text. This default
      value is adjustable. If adjusted, it could be dropped to 7 or 6.  It is rarely raised
      to 9 or 10.<br/><br/>
      
      Each of the characters has this confidence level. If any one of the characters
      has a confidence lower than the minimum confidence, it is highlighted to the
      verification operator when viewing the fields in the verify panel.  The operator
      can visually confirm or correct any misread values.<br/><br/>

      There are other ways to confirm characters are correct, including mechanisms
      to automatically lower or raise confidence values based on one or more conditions.
      The ValidationsAndTextAdjustments action library is one action library that
      can perform automatic verification.  Other mechanisms such as pattern matching
      or voting can also automatically adjust the confidence of characters
      within a field.<br/><br/>
      
      In some situations, a character's confidence may be represented
      in a range from 0-9. Be sure to understand the correct range when
      working with confidence values.<br/><br/>

      A field's confidence value can be returned as a string using the field property "CONFIDENCESTRING".
      For example, if the action rrCompare("@P\Field1.CONFIDENCESTRING","99999") is called on a page
      that has a field name "Field1", it will return true if the field contains 5 characters and
      confidence of the field is "99999".<br/><br/>

      <b>Smart Parameters</b><br/><br/>

      Parameters that use an "@" notation, such as "@X.xxxx", "@P.xxxx", "@STRING()", "@PILOT()", etc., are
      known as "smart parameters". The data passed as a parameter to an action will be evaluated at runtime.
      For example, "@X" is notation for accessing the current object. "@P" accesses the page object, which
      could be the current object, or a parent object. Being evaluated at runtime, allows recognized data
      or other batch specific data to be passed to actions or stored as metadata. A smart parameter such
      as @DATE could be used to name export files based on the current date.  Similar smart parameters
      will retrieve the current batch ID, current batch directory, current operator ID, and more.
      Smart parameters are a very powerful mechanism of getting data from other areas of the application and copying
      it into a new location or passing it as a parameter to an action. It is recommended that the smart parameter
      documentation is reviewed to understand the full capabilities available. The top-level help of the
      ValidationAndTextAdjustments action library also has information about smart parameters. Some characters such
      as +, \ and . also have special smart parameter meaning and may be interpreted as a smart parameter.  This
      can be controlled using smart parameter syntax.<br/><br/>
    </h>
    
    <method name="AddChildDCONode" qi=": Adds new Node (Document, Page or Field) to the Runtime DCO">
      <p name="ObjectType"/>
      <p name="ID"/>
      <p name="Type"/>
      <p name="Status"/>
      <p name="Index"/>
      <ap>        
        <b>ObjectType:</b> The new DCO node object type: 3=Field, 2=Page, 1=Document.<br/><br/>
        <b>ID:</b> The Node's ID. This value must be unique. If blank, a unique ID will be assigned.<br/><br/>
        <b>Type:</b> The Node's Type.<br/><br/>
        <b>Status:</b> The Node's Numeric Status. Optional, Defaults to 0.<br/><br/>
        <b>Index:</b> The Node's Zero based Index. Optional, Defaults to add to the end of the children (-1).<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Creates a child DCO node of the type indicated by the first argument (A Field, Page or Document).
        The new node is a child to the current DCO node.<br/><br/>
        
        If a node of the same type and ID exists no new node will be created and the action will return True.<br/><br/>
        
        Restrictions: Documents can only have child Documents, Pages and Fields.  Pages can only have child Pages, and Fields.
        Fields can only have child fields.<br/><br/>
        
        The new child node is added to the runtime DCO hierarchy. The new node will now function just like all existing
        nodes meaning that any rules defined for that object type in the setup DCO will now run in subsequent rules.
        For example, if a page is added to a document, subsequent page level rules associated with that object type
        will be run on this new node.<br/><br/>

        A page level node must have a unique ID but typically has common types.  For example, the ID of a page could
        be "TM000005" and the type could be "Main_Page".  It is common for fields to have a field ID that is the same
        as the field type, however the ID must be unique.
        <e>
          <b>AddChildDCONode("3","NewField","NewField","0","-1")</b><br/>
          This example adds a new field DCO node to the current object. 
          The new field is added to the end of the collection of children
          and has an initial status of 0.<br/><br/>

          <b>AddChildDCONode("@X.MyLevel", "@X.MyID", "@P\TM000001\TYPE", "@X.MyStatus", "@X.MyPosition")</b><br/>
          This example illustrates obtaining the parameter values at runtime.  If the parameters
          are static, then they can simply be hard coded in the parameter as in first example.
          Here the intent is to show that values can be obtained from other locations
          as necessary.  The object created is determined by the value of "MyLevel" on the current node.
          Likewise, the ID is determined by the value of the variable MyID on the current node.
          Assuming this action is called on a document node, the type obtained is the same type
          of the page with ID "TM000001" and that page's type is passed as a parameter to create
          a sibling page of the same type.  The last two parameters in this example are also
          obtained from variables on the current object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the node is successfully created.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for a list of status values.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="AddChildDCONodeToBatch" qi=": Adds new Node (Document, Page or Field) to the Runtime DCO on the batch level">
      <p name="ObjectType"/>
      <p name="ID"/>
      <p name="Type"/>
      <p name="Status"/>
      <p name="Index"/>
      <ap>
        <b>ObjectType:</b> The new DCO node object type: 3=Field, 2=Page, 1=Document. 
        If the parameter provided is invalid, this value will default to "3" (Field).<br/><br/>
        <b>ID:</b> The Node's ID. This value must be unique. If blank, a unique ID will be assigned.<br/><br/>
        <b>Type:</b> The Node's Type.<br/><br/>
        <b>Status:</b> The Node's Numeric Status. Optional, Defaults to 0.<br/><br/>
        <b>Index:</b> The Node's Zero based Index. Optional, Defaults to add to the end of the children (-1).<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Creates a child DCO node of the type indicated by the first argument (A Field, Page or Document).
        The new node is a child to the batch top-level DCO node.  The child will always be added to
        the batch object regardless of the calling object's level.<br/><br/>

        If a node of the same type and ID exists no new node will be created and the action will return True.<br/><br/>

        Restrictions: Documents can only have child Documents, Pages and Fields.  Pages can only have child Pages, and Fields.
        Fields can only have child fields.<br/><br/>

        The new child node is added to the runtime DCO hierarchy. The new node will now function just like all existing
        nodes meaning that any rules defined for that object type in the setup DCO will now run in subsequent rules.
        For example, if a page is added to a document, subsequent page level rules associated with that object type
        will be run on this new node.<br/><br/>

        A page level node must have a unique ID but can have common types.  For example, the ID of a page could
        be "TM000005" and the type could be "Main_Page".  It is common for fields to have a field ID that is the same
        as the field type, however the ID must be unique.
        <e>
          <b>AddChildDCONodeToBatch("3","NewField","NewField","0","-1")</b><br/>
          This example adds a new field DCO node to the batch object.
          The new field is added to the end of the collection of children
          and has an initial status of 0.<br/><br/>

          <b>AddChildDCONodeToBatch("@X.MyLevel", "@X.MyID", "@P\TM000001\TYPE", "@X.MyStatus", "@X.MyPosition")</b><br/>
          This example illustrates obtaining the parameter values at runtime.  If the parameters
          are static, then they can simply be hard coded in the parameter as in first example.
          Here the intent is to show that values can be obtained from other locations
          as necessary.  The object created is determined by the value of "MyLevel" on the current node.
          Likewise, the ID is determined by the value of the variable MyID on the current node.
          Assuming this action is called on a document node, the type obtained is the same type
          of the page with ID "TM000001" and that page's type is passed as a parameter to create
          a sibling page of the same type.  The last two parameters in this example are also
          obtained from variables on the current object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the node is successfully created.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for a list of status values.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    <method name="CheckDCOStatus" qi=": Confirms that the status of the Document Hierarchy's current object is identical to the status entered as the parameter.">
      <p name="ExpectedStatus"/>
      <ap>
        <b>ExpectedStatus:</b> The expected status of the current DCO object.
        Multiple expected status values can be provided as a comma separated list.
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Confirms that the status of the Document Hierarchy's current object is identical
        to any of the the status values entered as the parameter.<br/><br/>
        
        When only one status value is provided, 
        this action is equivalent to the following action:<br/>
        rrCompare("expected status","@X.STATUS")<br/>
        Either action will achieve the same result where "expected status" is
        the same value passed in to CheckDCOStatus when a single status value
        is expected.  It may be still desirable to
        use CheckDCOStatus for clarity of intention.
        If an object needs to be checked for multiple possible status values,
        then use CheckDCOStatus.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CheckDCOStatus("0")</b><br/>
          CopyFile("@PILOT(BATCHDIR)+\+@ID+.tif", "@APPPATH(export)+\+@ID+.tif", true)
          This action returns true if the current object has a
          status equal to 0, and false if it does not.
          This kind of action is used to control logic flow. The action after this action
          will run if true is returned.  If false is returned, then the next action is
          skipped and control continues on the next function in the ruleset.
          In this particular example, if the status of the current object is 0 then
          the CopyFile action is run to and the current image file 
          is copied to the export directory. Any set of required
          actions can follow a check like this.<br/><br/>

          <b>CheckDCOStatus("0,2,49")</b><br/>          
          This action returns true if the current object has a
          status equal to 0 or 2 or 49, and false if it does not.<br/><br/>
          
          rrSet("48", "@X.StatusToCheck")<br/>
          <b>CheckDCOStatus("@X.StatusToCheck")</b><br/>
          This is similar to the above example however the expected status
          is stored in a DCO variable called "StatusToCheck".  In this example,
          the variable was previously set to "48" so the action will check if the
          current DCO object has a status of 48 and returns true if the current object has a status
          equal to 48, and false if it does not.<br/><br/>          
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the DCO status matches the parameter value. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetDCOStatus, CheckDCOType, rrCompare<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information about an object's status.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="CheckDCOType" qi=": Confirms that the status of the Document Hierarchy's current object is identical to the status entered as the parameter.">
      <p name="ExpectedType"/>
      <ap>
        <b>ExpectedType:</b> The expected type of the current DCO object.
        Multiple expected status values can be provided as a comma separated list.
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Confirms that the type of the Document Hierarchy's current object is identical
        to any of the the type values entered as the parameter.<br/><br/>

        When only one type value is provided,
        this action is equivalent to the following action:<br/>
        rrCompare("expected type","@X.TYPE")<br/>
        Either action will achieve the same result where "expected type" is
        the same value passed in to CheckDCOType when a single type value
        is expected.  It may be still desirable to
        use CheckDCOType for clarity of intention.
        If an object needs to be checked for multiple possible type values,
        then use CheckDCOType.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CheckDCOType("Invoice")</b><br/>
          CopyFile("@PILOT(BATCHDIR)+\+@ID+.tif", "@APPPATH(export)+\+@ID+.tif", true)<br/>
          This action returns true if the current object has a
          type equal to Invoice, and false if it does not.
          This kind of action is used to control logic flow. The action after this action
          will run if true is returned.  If false is returned, then the next action is
          skipped and control continues on the next function in the ruleset.
          In this particular example, if the type of the current object is Invoice then
          the CopyFile action is run to and the current image file
          is copied to the export directory. Any set of required
          actions can follow a check like this.<br/><br/>

          <b>CheckDCOType("Invoice,Main_Page,Trailing_Page")</b><br/>
          This action returns true if the current object has a
          type equal to Invoice or Main_Page or Trailing_Page, and false if it does not.<br/><br/>

          rrSet("Main_Page", "@X.TypeToCheck")<br/>
          <b>CheckDCOType("@X.TypeToCheck")</b><br/>
          This is similar to the above example however the expected type
          is stored in a DCO variable called "TypeToCheck".  In this example,
          the variable was previously set to Main_Page so the action will check if the
          current DCO object has a type of Main_Page and returns true if the current object has a type
          equal to Main_Page, and false if it does not.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the DCO status matches the parameter value. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetDCOStatus, CheckDCOType, rrCompare<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for more information about an object's type.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="CheckDocumentCount" qi=": Compares the number of documents to a previously set variable.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        This action counts the number of documents 
        within the batch and confirms that it matches the adjusted or expected
        number of documents.  A single document can contain multiple pages,
        but will still be counted as one document.<br/><br/>

        The number of expected documents is usually provided by the operator of a job's Scan task.
        This very handy action can compare the actual amount to the estimate at any time after a
        <b>CreateDocuments</b> action has assembled the documents in the batch.<br/>

        The action will check the count against an "adjusted" count or an "expected" count.  The
        adjusted count is determined by the value of the batch level variable "AD".
        If the batch level variable "AD" does not exist or is not a valid number, the actual pages
        count from the batch pilot object is used.  It is expected that a previous rule or task
        as set the AD variable at the batch level as appropriate for the application logic.<br/><br/>

        if the adjusted count, @B.AD, is not set, or is zero, then the batch page count
        is compared to the "expected" page count.  The expected page count is determined
        by the value of the batch level variable "ED".
        If the batch level variable "ED" does not exist or is not a valid number, the expected pages
        count from the batch pilot object is used.<br/><br/>

        If both the adjusted count and expected counts are non-zero, then this action will
        compare the number of pages against the expected count.<br/><br/>

        Refer to the CreateDocuments action to understand how it converts the pages in a
        batch to a document structure after each page has had a page type configured
        in a typical Page ID task.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          rrSet("10","@B.AD")<br/>
          <b>CheckPageCount()</b><br/><br/>

          In this example, if the actual number of documents in the batch is 10,
          then the action will return true, otherwise it will return false.
          It does not matter if a document has one page or many pages,
          it will still be counted as one document.<br/><br/>

          rrSet("12","@B.ED")<br/>
          rrSet("10","@B.AD")<br/>
          <b>CheckPageCount()</b><br/><br/>

          In this example,
          because both ED and AD are set, the count will be checked against
          the ED setting.<br/><br/>
        </e>
        
      </h>
      <lvl>Batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the actual count is the same as the expected or adjusted count.
        Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
    </method>
    
    
    <method name="CheckFieldConfidence" qi=": Checks the confidence of all fields on child pages against a minimum acceptable confidence value and sets the page status.">
      <p name="MinimumConfidence"/>
      <p name="NewPageStatus"/>
      <p name="PageStatusToCheck"/>
      <ap>        
        <b>MinimumConfidence:</b> The Numeric value of the minimum confidence required for the action to return true.
        The confidence must be an integer between 1 and 10.
        If a field has the variable "ReqConf" set, the value of this variable will override
        the provided parameter. The "ReqConf" variable uses the range 0-9.
        <br/><br/>
        
        <b>NewPageStatus:</b> The Numeric Page Status code to assign to any page that has one or 
        more fields with Low Confidence data: if a field's ConfidenceString property contains 
        a value lower than the first parameter.  Child fields, line items, etc. are included.  
        Typically, "1" (Problem) is the value of this parameter.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>PageStatusToCheck:</b> A comma separated list of page status to check.  
        If blank then only pages with a status of "0" will be checked.  Pages that already
        have a page status of "1" will be skipped even if it is specified as a parameter.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Checks the confidence of all field data on child pages with a status that matches the specified status.
        Each field will have the confidence value of each character within the field evaluated 
        against a minimum acceptable confidence value.
        1 is the lowest confidence level and 10 is the highest confidence.<br/><br/>
        
        Starting from the current DCO node, the action will check each field to confirm each character meets
        the minimum confidence.  If any field contain characters that have a confidence 
        lower than the specified minimum, the parent page status is changed
        to the new status provided by the parameter.  If this action is called on a page object,
        then just the fields on the page object will be checked.  If called on a document, then all
        pages within that document node are checked.  If called at the batch level, then all pages
        in the entire batch are checked.<br/><br/>
        
        If a field object has the variable "ReqConf" set, then the value of
        that variable will override the value of the MinimumConfidence parameter for the field.<br/><br/>
        
        A typical use for this action is to confirm that the recognized field data
        is of a high enough confidence so the application can assume that the page data has been
        recognized accurately.  If one or more characters in any field attached to the page have a confidence
        that is lower than the minimum, then the page status is changed. Typically the status is changed
        to "1" so the page will be flagged for review by a verify operator in a verification panel. 
        During verification, the operator will review and adjust the recognized data as necessary,
        raising the confidence so the page will result in a successful status allowing it to complete
        the verification step.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CheckFieldConfidence("8", "1", "")</b><br/>
          This example will confirm that all fields contain characters that have a confidence
          of 8, 9 or 10.  If one character of any field has a lower confidence, then the parent
          page will be set to a status of "1".  Only pages with a status of "0" will be checked.<br/><br/>

          rrSet("1","@X.TheNewStatus")<br/>
          <b>CheckFieldConfidence("6", "@X.TheNewStatus", "0,2,49")</b><br/>
          This is similar to the above example.  Here the action will check that
          each character on a page has a confidence between 6 and 10.  If a field has
          a lower confidence, then the parent page will be set to the value provided
          in the DCO variable "TheNewStatus", which was preset to "1".  Pages with
          a status of 0, 2 or 49 will be checked.  If a page does not match this status,
          the page's fields will not be checked.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        Batch, Document or Page.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if all fields in all source pages meet the confidence requirement.<br/>
        <b>False</b> if any field on any page has low confidence data, or if the parameters are not Numeric.
      </ret>
      <see>
        CheckDCOStatus<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help of this action library for more information regarding confidence.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="CheckIntegrity" qi=": Checks that the batch meets the document page count requirements.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Checks that the integrity of the batch, as detailed in the Page file
        of the current task, meets the integrity requirements (min, max, order) set within the
        Document Hierarchy (Setup DCO).<br/><br/>

        "Integrity" refers to the correct types and numbers of pages within each document
        in the batch and the correct order of the pages in each document.<br/><br/>

        This action performs an integrity check on the entire batch regardless of the DCO level on which it is called.<br/><br/>

        Refer to the CreateDocuments action to understand how it converts the pages in a
        batch to a document structure after each page has had a page type configured
        in a typical Page ID task.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CheckIntegrity()</b><br/>
          This action is typically called at a batch level after the document structure has
          been created by the "Create Documents" action to confirm the objects
          within the batch meet the integrity requirements.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level, but usually called at the batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Returns <b>True</b> if no integrity problems are found.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        See the top level help for more information regarding the DCO.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    
    <method name="CheckLastDCOType" qi=": Checks the type of the previous sibling object.">
      <p name="PreviousTypeToMatch"/>
      <p name="LevelToCheck"/>
      <p name="IgnoreTypes"/>
      <ap>        
        <b>PreviousTypeToMatch:</b> The previous object's DCO Type to compare. The comparison is case sensitive.<br/><br/>

        <b>LevelToCheck:</b> (Optional) Objects to compare:<br/>
        0 - All<br/>
        1 - Documents only<br/>
        2 - Pages only<br/>
        3 - Fields only<br/><br/>
        
        If this parameter is blank or invalid, it will default to 0.<br/><br/>

        <b>IgnoreTypes:</b> (Optional) A comma-delimited list of Types to skip/ignore in the evaluation.  
        If the prior object's Type is listed
        within the parameter then it will be not be evaluated and if present, 
        the next object prior to it will be evaluated to see if it matches the specified type.
        The first previous sibling object not within this list will 
        be checked to see if its type matches the expected type.<br/><br/>
        
        If the parameter is blank, then only the previous object will be checked to see if it
        matches the expected type.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action allows you to test the previous sibling DCO Type relative
        to the current DCO node
        so an application can take specific subsequent steps based on that type.
        This previous node is also considered to be the "last" object
        that was processed prior to the current DCO object that is being
        processed by the rules engine.<br/><br/>
        
        The type of the previous sibling object is compared to the type
        provided in the parameter. If the type of the previous sibling DCO node exactly matches
        the specified type, then the action will return true.<br/><br/>
        
        The LevelToCheck parameter determines if every sibling should be checked or
        if only specific sibling types should be checked. If the previous sibling level
        does not match the level to check, then the action will look backwards through
        the previous siblings until it finds a DCO node with the correct level to check. 
        For example, if there previous node is a page but the level to check
        is specified as document nodes, then the page node will be skipped and
        the search will continue until the first sibling node is found that
        matches the expected level is found.<br/><br/>
        
        If one or more types to skip has been provided, the previous sibling DCO node
        will not be checked to see if it is a matching type if the type is specified
        in the skip parameter list.  For example, if looking for objects of the level
        of "page" and the previous sibling found is a page and the type of the page
        is listed in the skip list, then that node will not be compared and instead
        the search will continue to look at the next previous sibling.<br/><br/>   
        
        This action only checks sibling objects that are in the DCO prior to the 
        currently running object.  For example, if the current object
        is a page level object and the previous search reaches the
        first page in the current document, the search will not continue to
        the previous document in the DCO hierarchy.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CheckLastDCOType("Separator", "", "")</b><br/>
          SetPageType("Invoice")<br/>
          SetPageStatus("1")<br/><br/>

          Assuming this action is called on a Page level DCO object, 
          this sequence checks to see if the previous sibling object was a Separator page.
          If so, the type and status of the current page are set to Invoice and 1.
          If the previous sibling object's type was not a "Separator", the subsequent actions will not execute.
          If the previous sibling object's type is "Separator", then the SetPageType action will run next.<br/><br/>
          
          Note that this example does not limit checks to page level sibling objects only, 
          to do that it would set the second parameter to 2.<br/><br/>

          <b>CheckLastDCOType("Separator", "", "Trailing_Page,Main_Page")</b><br/>
          For this example, assume the batch has this document object
          that contains these pages:<br/><br/>
          
          ˂Document><br/>
          - ˂Main_Page><br/>
          - ˂Separator><br/>
          - ˂Trailing_Page><br/>
          - ˂Trailing_Page><br/>
          - ˂Invoice><br/>
          - ˂EOB><br/>
          - ˂Trailing_Page><br/><br/>
          
          If the action shown is called on the "Invoice" page, then the action
          would find at the first Trailing_Page and ignore it because
          it is in the ignore list. It would then look at the node before
          it which is another Trailing_Page and it would also be ignored.
          The action would then look at the previous sibling of "Separator"
          and return True because that previous sibling is not skipped and it matches
          the expected type.<br/><br/>
          
          As another example, assume that the action is called on the
          EOB object.  The action would look at the previous sibling
          "Invoice".  This type is not ignored so the action would
          see if it is the expected type "Separator".  Because the
          previous sibling is not "Separator", the action will return false.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Document, Page or Field.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the Type property of the Document Hierarchy's previous sibling non-skipped object
        matches the page type attempting to be matched.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetDCOType, CheckDCOType<br/>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about an object's type.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="CheckPageCount" qi=": Confirms the number of pages in the batch meets the expected count.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Counts the number of pages within the batch and confirms that it matches the adjusted or expected
        number of pages.<br/><br/>

        The action will check the count against an "adjusted" count or an "expected" count.  The
        adjusted count is determined by the value of the batch level variable "AP".
        If the batch level variable "AP" does not exist or is not a valid number, the actual pages
        count from the batch pilot object is used.  It is expected that a previous rule or task
        as set the AP variable at the batch level as appropriate for the application logic.<br/><br/>

        if the adjusted count is not set, or is zero, then the batch page count
        is compared to the "expected" page count.  The expected page count is determined
        by the value of the batch level variable "EP".
        If the batch level variable "EP" does not exist or is not a valid number, the expected pages
        count from the batch pilot object is used.<br/><br/>

        If both the adjusted count and expected counts are non-zero, then this action will
        compare the number of pages against the adjusted count.  If the adjusted count
        is zero, then this action will check the current number of pages against
        the expected count.<br/><br/>
        
        Refer to the CreateDocuments action to understand how it converts the pages in a
        batch to a document structure after each page has had a page type configured
        in a typical Page ID task.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          rrSet("10","@B.AP")<br/>
          <b>CheckPageCount()</b><br/><br/>
          
          In this example, if the actual number of pages in the batch is 10,
          then the action will return true, otherwise it will return false.<br/><br/>

          rrSet("12","@B.EP")<br/>
          rrSet("10","@B.AP")<br/>
          <b>CheckPageCount()</b><br/><br/>
          
          In this example,
          because both EP and AP are set, the count will be checked against
          the AP setting.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the actual count is the same as the expected or adjusted count.
        Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
    </method>

      <method name="ClearVariableInFields" qi=": Copies the value of the field along with the position and confidence.">
          <p name="VariableName" type="string"/>
          <p name="FieldPrefix" type="string"/>
          <ap>
              <b>VariableName:</b> The variable to clear in all child fields.<br/><br/>

              <b>FieldPrefix:</b> Optional.  If provided, only fields that match this type prefix will be cleared.<br/><br/>

              Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action clears a specific variable in all child fields for the current page.  If a FieldPrefix is provided,
              then the action only clears the variable if the field type starts with the text
              provided in the FieldPrefix.
              <br/><br/>
              <e>
                  <b>ClearVariableInFields("FlexFieldType","Field")</b><br/><br/>

                  This example clears the variable "FlexFieldType" in
                  all child fields that have the type "FieldXXXXXXXX".<br/><br/>                  
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Page.</lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>
              Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy.
          </see>
      </method>      
      

    <method name="CopyFieldAndChildren" qi=": Copies an entire field object to another page or field.">
      <p name="Target"/>
      <p name="Source"/>      
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> Specifies the parent page or field object where the new field will be added. It must
        consist of smart parameter syntax that navigates to the target page / field object or it can be the name of the page
        where the child field will be copied.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>Source:</b> Specifies the field object to copy as a child to the target object. This must be a smart parameter navigation specification
        for a field object.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action copies the source field and all of its child fields, if any, to the destination object.
        The field's text values and runtime DCO variables and children are also copied.<br/><br/>
        
        The target object cannot be a child of the source object.<br/><br/>

        If the target object already contains a child field of the same name, the existing child field will be removed
        and will be replaced by the new field and its children.<br/><br/>        
        <e>
          <b>CopyFieldAndChildren("TM000002", "@X")</b><br/>
          This example assumes the action is part of a rule called on the field that will be copied.
          The current field object, specified by the smart parameter syntax "@X", and any child fields
          is copied to the page with the ID "TM000002". If the current field object is called "MyField",
          then "MyField", and any children field objects, will be copied to the page with ID TM000002.<br/><br/>

          rrSet("TM000002", "@P.MyTarget")<br/>
          <b>CopyFieldAndChildren("@P.MyTarget", "@X")</b><br/>
          This example is very similar to the previous example.  The difference is that the target page name
          is stored within a DCO variable.  In this case, the DCO variable is called "MyTarget" and
          is a variable on the parent page of the field attached to this rule.
          In a realistic scenario, the variable "MyTarget" would have populated in a previous rule or
          task and is now being used by CopyFieldAndChildren.  This example sets the variable
          immediately before calling the action for illustration of how the page name can be stored
          in a variable.<br/><br/>

          <b>CopyFieldAndChildren("@D\Main_Page", "@P\MyFieldToCopy")</b><br/>
          This example assumes the action is part of a rule called on a page that contains a field called "MyFieldToCopy"
          or on a child field of that page. The example also assumes that the source page is within the same document structure
          that contains a Main_Page.
          The smart parameter "@P\MyFieldToCopy" instructs the action to access the field "MyFieldToCopy" from
          the current page.  The smart parameter "@D\Main_Page" instructs the action to locate the "Main_Page"
          object which is a child of the parent document object.
          The result is that the field "MyFieldToCopy" and any child fields it contains, if any, 
          are copied to the "Main_Page" of the current parent document.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        This action can be called on any level as but is typically called on the 
        current page object that contains the field to be copied or 
        on the current field to be copied.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        True if the operation succeeds.  False if an error occurs.
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about DCO variables.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    <method name="CountPagesToVariable" qi=": Counts the number of pages in the document.">
      <p name="Variable"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Variable:</b> The variable that will hold the page count. If the input is a smart parameter
        syntax that specifies a variable, the value will be stored in that location.  If the input
        is not a smart parameter, it will be treated as the name of the variable to
        use on the current DCO object.<br/>        
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action will count the number of page objects that exist on the current 
        DCO document object and write the result into the variable.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CountPagesToVariable("MyVarName")</b><br/>
          This example will place the count in a variable called "MyVarName" on the current
          document object.  If the variable does not exist in the DCO, it will be created.<br/><br/>

          <b>CountPagesToVariable("@B.MyVarName")</b><br/>
          This example uses smart parameter syntax and 
          will place the count in a variable called "MyVarName" on the batch level
          object.  If the variable does not exist in the DCO, it will be created.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Document level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about DCO variables.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    
    <method name="CreateDocuments" qi=": Arranges the contents of a batch into documents based on the Document Integrity rules.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>        
        This action assembles all of the pages within the batch to a hierarchy of Batch->Document->Page.
        Pages within the batch are grouped together based on the document integrity rules based
        in the settings in the Setup DCO.  The special document and page level variables/properties
        "min", "max" and "order", along with the order of the pages within the batch, are
        used to determine how many pages, and which pages, belong under 
        a single document node.  The rules for how the pages become arranged can be complex
        and these variables must be properly set in the Setup DCO.  The result is that
        the pages are arranged in the runtime DCO in the manner specified.<br/><br/>
        
        Initially pages are ingested to a batch with a page type of "Other", which is a page
        without a document structure and each page is attached directly to the batch node.
        After ingestion, images could be manipulated such as splitting single page tifs
        out of a PDF or from a multi-page tif. The parent fields may then be marked as deleted
        so they are no longer processed, although they can still exist in the batch for export
        at the end of the process. Images may then have image enhancements applied to 
        clean them up and make them consistent for the page identification step.<br/><br/>
        
        Next page identification would occur.  Rules run on each page of type "Other". These
        custom rules then determine the actual page type of the document. It could be determined
        by matching an image fingerprint to assign a the real type, reading a barcode on the
        actual page or on the preceding page, to determine the correct type, other types of automated tests
        can be performed, or pages can be manually identified with the Flex ID task.  Once
        the correct type for a page is determined, the page type is changed from "Other"
        to the correct type.  This is important as the next step will be to create the document
        structure.  The <b>CreateDocuments</b> action will look at these assigned page types
        and generate the desired object hierarchy based on the order, page types, and the
        special "min", "max" and "order" variables.<br/><br/>        
        
        A high-level example of how this action can work would can be illustrated with
        the foundation APT application. In this application, a single document can contain
        one "Main_Page" type followed by any number of "Trailing_Page" types.  Separators can also
        be optionally used to note the start and end of documents.  The order in which the
        pages exist within the batch is also very important as it determines how pages are associated
        with the same document. A document can consist of only 1 Main_Page and no trailing pages
        or it can have any number of trailing pages.  As CreateDocuments runs, it will
        look at each page type, the controlling variables and organize each page under a document.
        The first document may have under it 1 Main_Page and 2 Trailing_Page. This is a 3
        page document.  The next page type was Main_Page and since only 1 is allowed,
        create documents created a new document node and put the page in the new document node
        along with the trailing pages, if any.  This continues until all of the pages
        have been arranged.<br/><br/>
        
        When developing an application, the runtime DCO is written to the batch directory
        for use by the next step in the workflow. This XML can be reviewed by the 
        application developer to confirm that the application is configured correctly so
        the pages are arranged correctly under documents.  While the developer can
        manually review the XML file, it is only for direct use by the systems within
        Datacap.  Any direct manipulation by custom actions or other applications
        is not supported.  If writing custom actions, the supported DCO objects
        and Datacap APIs can be used to manipulate the DCO and its nodes.<br/><br/>

        Batches containing existing document structures will cause this action to return False,
        with no affect to the existing document structure.  If it is necessary to 
        re-create the batch structure a second time, then the existing document structure
        needs to be removed prior to calling this action. This can be performed
        with the action RemoveDocumentStructure.<br/><br/>

        Note: During document creation, temporary IDs are assigned (with a different format than final Document IDs), 
        and if the action fails, these temporary IDs may remain.<br/><br/>

        This action is applied at the Batch level, and generally in its own Ruleset such as a CreateDocuments ruleset.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CreateDocuments()</b>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if successful. Otherwise, <b>False.</b><br/><br/>
        
        This action will not create a document structure if a document structure already exists.
        If there is a need to re-create the document structure, first remove the document structure
        then call this action.<br/>
      </ret>
      <see>
        RemoveDocumentStructure<br/>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document structure.
      </see>
    </method>
      
    
    <method name="CreateFields" qi=": Creates the fields for a page and the associated runtime DCO Data file.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Creates the runtime field objects based on the field structure defined
        for the page within the setup DCO. Once the fields have been created,
        then the rules engine will begin running rules on fields and operations
        such as recognition can be performed on the fields.<br/><br/>
        
        This action creates the field DCO structure.  It does not load the position
        information for the fields. A fields "zone" or "position" is determined by the
        fingerprint that is associated with the page.  A single page type can have one
        or more fingerprints that are applicable to the page. For example, a page could be
        an "Invoice" type. Invoices have a known set of data that is captured such as
        Date, Invoice Number, Customer, Vendor, Tax, Total, etc. An invoice from vendor
        "XYZ Plumbing" probably has a very different format than an invoice from "123 Donuts".
        Different fingerprint templates will identify the "zone" coordinates for each field
        on the different documents, allowing documents of different formats to be processed
        by a single application with the same set of rules. Additional fingerprints allows
        an application to ingest and validate invoices from other vendors without 
        having to develop new rules to process the new type of document.<br/><br/>
        
        When this action is run and the fields are created, they exist in the 
        batch as an XML datafile. The Data file for the first page
        in the batch, tm000001, as an example, is tm000001.xml.  While developing
        an application it may be useful to look at this file to debug problems.
        It is not recommended and is not supported for custom actions or external
        program to directly
        use or manipulate the runtime DCO XML file. The only way a custom action is allowed
        to manipulate DCO nodes is through the Datacap DCO object interface.<br/><br/>

        This Data file lists all fields for the current page based on the fields listed
        in the setup Document Hierarchy. Each field has an ID and 
        three properties with default values:
        TYPE, Position, and STATUS. If this action is called but fields are not
        created, then it is recommended to confirm that the action is being
        called on the correct page object and that the setup DCO is correct.<br/><br/>

        Subsequent actions of various kinds such as Recognition, Locate, Zone, Validation, etc.,
        can assign other values to these properties.  
        These actions can also add properties, also called DCO variables,
        and values to the Data file, or remove properties and values.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          AnalyzeImage()<br/>          
          SetSearchArea("0.5")<br/>
          SetProblemValue("0.3")<br/>
          SetDirectoryFPX("@APPPATH(fingerprint)")<br/>
          FindFingerprint("True")<br/>
          <b>CreateFields()</b><br/><br/>

          This Invoices application sequence sets up the current page for processing,
          associates the page with a fingerprint,
          and finally creates the fields and associated runtime DCO with blank fields for the page.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if successful. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for this action library for information regarding application
        objects, the DCO hierarchy, variables and how they can be used.<br/>
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="CopyFieldTextConfidenceAndPosition" qi=": Copies the value of the field along with the position and confidence.">
      <p name="Source" type="string"/>
      <p name="Target" type="string"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Source:</b> A reference to the source field.<br/><br/>

        <b>Target:</b> A reference to the target field.<br/><br/>

        Either parameter is optional.  If a parameter is not specified, it will default to the text value of the
        calling object.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        The action retrieves the value, confidence, zone coordinates and image reference of the source field object,
        and copies them to the target field object.  The target must specify a field object.
        <br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CopyFieldTextConfidenceAndPosition("@B\OPERATOR","@P\OPERATOR")</b><br/><br/>

          This example copies the Batch field OPERATOR values to the
          OPERATOR field of the bound object of the Document Hierarchy.  It implies that this action is called on a page
          object and the child field OPERATOR will be the target field.<br/><br/>

          <b>CopyFieldAndConfidence("@P\Total","@X")</b><br/><br/>

          This example assumes the rule is associated with a field object.  The values from the sibling
          field object "Total" will be copied to the current field object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level but the parameters must reference a field using smart parameter notation.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the action cannot retrieve the target or source object. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
        If the position or confidence cannot be copied, the action will still return <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy.
      </see>
    </method>
    

    <method name="CopyFieldAndPositionToVariable" qi=": Copies the value of the field and position to another variable in the same field object for all child objects.">
      <p name="TextVariable" type="string"/>
      <p name="PositionVariable" type="string"/>
      <ap>
        <b>TextVariable:</b> The name of the variable to store the text value. If blank, then the text value will not be stored.<br/><br/>

        <b>PositionVariable:</b> The name of the variable to store the position. If blank, then the position will not be stored.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for both variables.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action will check the current object and all children objects.  If the object is a field
        object, then the text value of the field will be stored in a backup variable whose name is specified by the
        TextVariable parameter and the position of the field will be backed up into a variable whose name is
        specified by the PositionVariable.  Variables will be created using the provided name on the current field
        object, if this action is called on a field, and each field child field object of the current object. <br/><br/>
        
        This action can be useful to back up field information on an entire page. For example, the action could
        be called prior to a Verify task and then after the verification is complete, the application can check to see
        if the data as changed in any the fields.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CopyFieldAndPositionToVariable("PreVerify Val", "PreVerify Pos")</b><br/><br/>

          Assuming this action is called on a document object, the action will check all fields on every
          page within the document object.  For each field on every page, the text value of the field will be duplicated
          in a variable called "PreVerify Val" on the same object and the field position will be stored
          in a variable called "PreVerify Pos".  For example, if the document contained two pages and
          each page contained three fields, then each field on each page will have the new
          variable "PreVerify Val" and "PreVerify Pos" created with the data from the field.<br/><br/>

          rrSet("PreVerify Val", "@X.MyTextVarName")<br/>
          rrSet("PreVerify Pos", "@X.MyPosVarName")<br/>
          <b>CopyFieldAndPositionToVariable("@X.MyTextVarName", "@X.MyPosVarName")</b><br/><br/>

          This second example will have the same result as the first example.  This is simply an illustration
          of obtaining the names of the variables at runtime using smart parameters.  In an actual implementation
          the names would probably have been set by some other rule or obtained from a location that was
          set at runtime.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy.
      </see>
    </method>

      

    <method name="DeleteDCOChildOfType" qi=": Deletes a child DCO node of the specified type.">
      <p name="Type"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Type:</b> The child type to remove.
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Deletes all child objects of the specified type from the Document Hierarchy.
        The search is only 1 level deep which will only delete immediate child objects. 
        Grandchildren are not evaluated but will be removed if their parent is removed.<br/><br/>

        This action must be the last action called in a ruleset. Due to the removal of objects
        from the DCO, subsequent actions may not run on objects that were not deleted.  Any
        subsequent actions must be run in a following ruleset.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteDCOChildOfType("Trailing_Page")</b><br/>
          Assuming the application has pages of type called "Trailing_Page", when called
          at on a document level object, any children of type "Trailing_Page" will
          be removed from the parent object. Subsequent rules will no longer be run
          on the object that was removed.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Document, Page or Field.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the child objects do not exist. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="DeleteDCOChildWithStatus" qi=": Deletes a child DCO node of the specified status.">
      <p name="Status"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Status:</b> Remove all children with the specified status.
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Deletes all child objects with the specified status from the Document Hierarchy.
        The search is only 1 level deep which will only delete immediate child objects.
        Grandchildren are not evaluated but will be removed if their parent is removed.<br/><br/>
        
        This action must be the last action called in a ruleset. Due to the removal of objects
        from the DCO, subsequent actions may not run on objects that were not deleted.  Any
        subsequent actions must be run in a following ruleset.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteDCOChildWithStatus("75")</b><br/>
          While any status can be specified, 75 typically means that the object 
          has been previously marked for deletion.  The example
          will delete any children of with a status of 75
          from the parent object. Subsequent rules will no longer be run
          on the object that was removed.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Document, Page or Field.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> unless an error occurs then <b>False</b> is returned.
        If no child objects are removed because none of them have a matching
        status, the action will still return true.
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy and an object's status.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="DeleteDCOParent" qi=": Deletes the parent DCO node of the current node.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Deletes the parent of the document hierarchy object to which a rule with this action is bound.<br/><br/>
        
        This action will also delete the current object as well.  This must be called as
        the last action on a ruleset and that any subsequent actions
        that need to run must be placed in a following ruleset.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteDCOParent()</b><br/>
          If called on a page object, this will delete the parent object, which is
          usually a document node.  If called on
          a field node, this will delete the parent which is typically a page node.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Document, Page, and Field.  The calling object must have a parent node and a grandparent node.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if a parent object cannot be found, the grandparent cannot be found or if the deletion
        of the parent object fails.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="DeleteDCOChildrenWithVariableValue" qi=": Removes immediate children that contain a variable with a specific value.">
        <p name="VariableName" qi="The variable to test on the child object."/>
        <p name="VariableValue" qi="The value that indicates the child is to be deleted."/>
      <ap>
        All parameters support smart parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
          This action removes immediate children from the batch object, such as pages that have the batch object as the parent. 
          The child object is removed if it contains the specified DCO variable and the variable's value matches the provided value.<br/><br/>

          One use for this action is that as objects are processed, they can have a variable set that indicates
          they are no longer needed and should be removed from the DCO tree.  For example, PDF or multi-page TIFF
          documents that have been burst into single pages.  This action does not delete any of the files
          associated with the object, it only removes the object from the DCO tree so it is no longer processed.
          Any files that were associated with the object will remain in the batch directory.<br/><br/>

          The action is expected to run at the batch level and will only look at the immediate children, meaning
          that it does not traverse down document hierarchies to delete pages.
          Therefore, this action must be called prior to calling the CreateDocuments action which creates the full
          document tree structure.<br/><br/>

          It is recommended that this action is called only in the last function of a ruleset.<br/><br/>          
          <e>              
              <b>DeleteDCOChildrenWithVariableValue("original_source_image", "yes")</b><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
    </method>
      

    <method name="DeleteDCOChildren" qi=": Deletes all child objects from the calling object of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Deletes all child objects such as pages or fields that are children of the bound object of the Document
        Hierarchy. If called on a page object, this action will also remove the page data file (.xml),
        which holds the field information, from the batch,
        if it exists.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteDCOChildren()</b><br/>
          If this action were called on a document node, then all of the pages attached
          to the document, and their field objects, will be removed from the DCO.  If called
          on a page level object, then all of the fields will be removed from the page.
          If called on a field, then any children sub-fields will be removed.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if successful, otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="DeleteDCOChildrenAndVariables" qi=": Clears all variables and child objects of the current object.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Removes all DCO children and variable references from the currently bound object.  All child
        objects are deleted and all variables in the current DCO object are deleted, leaving the
        current DCO object existing, but it will not contain any data.<br/><br/>

        Once a child object is deleted, it no longer exists in the object hierarchy and
        rules will not be run on deleted objects.<br/><br/>

        While not explicitly prevented, this action is not expected to be called at the
        batch level because every object and variable from within the batch will be removed.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          CreateFields()<br/>
          <b>DeleteDCOChildrenAndVariables()</b><br/><br/>

          Applied at the Page level, the example will first add fields to the page and then remove
          all of the fields and all of the variables within the current page object.  While
          these two actions would not typically be ordered like this in a real application,
          it is intended to demonstrate that all of a page's field objects will be removed
          by this action.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if all child objects and their variables are removed, otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="DeleteDCOVariable" qi=": Deletes the specified DCO variable">
      <p name="VariableName" type="string"/>
      <p name="ObjectType" type="string"/>
      <ap>
        <b>VariableName:</b> The name of the variable to delete.<br/><br/>

        <b>ObjectType:</b> Indicates if the variable should be deleted on the current object
        or on children of a specific type.  If the value is invalid or blank, the current object
        will be used.<br/><br/>

        0 = Current object only.<br/>
        1 = Child document objects only.<br/>
        2 = Child page objects only.<br/>
        3 = Child field objects only.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action will delete the specified variable from the runtime DCO.  If the ObjectType is set to the
        current object, then the variable will be deleted for just the current object.  If a document,
        page or field object type is specified, then if the current object, or any of the child objects,
        match the specified object type, then the specified variable will be deleted.<br/><br/>

        It is not required that the variable exists on the target object, but if it does exist it will
        be deleted.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DeleteDCOVariable("MyVar","3")</b><br/><br/>

          This example will delete the variable "MyVar" on the current object, if it is a field,
          and all child objects. If this was called on a document object, then all of the fields
          on all pages within the current document would be checked and the variable would
          be deleted.<br/><br/>

          rrSet("MyVar", "@X.NameOfVarToDelete")<br/>
          rrSet("3", "@X.ObjectTypeToUpdate")<br/>
          <b>DeleteDCOVariable("@X.NameOfVarToDelete","@X.ObjectTypeToUpdate")</b><br/><br/>

          This second example has the same results as the first example.  It simply illustrates that
          the values can be passed using smart parameters. In an actual implementation, the variables
          would like be assigned in some previous rule or task.<br/><br/>

          <b>DeleteDCOVariable("MyVar", "0")</b><br/><br/>

          This example deletes the variable "MyVar", but only on the current object that is
          associated with the running rule.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy.
      </see>
    </method>
    

    <method name="HasDCOChildOfType" qi=": Returns true if the current object has a child of the specified type.">
      <p name="Type"/>
      <ap>
        The type to locate as a child of the current DCO node. Smart parameters are supported. <br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action checks the child objects of the current node to see if any of them
        match the type that is specified.  This action only checks the immediate child objects
        with a one child depth and will not check the children of the child nodes.<br/><br/>        
        <e>
          <b>HasDCOChildOfType("Main_Page")</b><br/>
          If the current DCO node has a child page object of the type "Main_Page", the
          action will return true.  If it does not, it will return false.<br/><br/>
          
          rrSet("Airline", "@X.LookForMe")
          <b>HasDCOChildOfType("@X.LookForMe")</b><br/>
          This example checks each of the child node to see if one of them
          is of the type specified in the DCO variable "LookForMe".  In this example,
          the variable was previously set with "Airline" causing the action
          to look for a child of type "Airline".<br/><br/>          
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the current object contains a child or children specified of the type
        specified by the parameter.  Otherwise, <b>False.</b><br/><br/>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information about the document hierarchy and an object's type.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="IsDocumentCountMoreThan" qi=": Counts the number of documents and returns true or false if the count is exceeded.">
      <p name="Count"/>
      <p name="ReturnTrueIfMore"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Count:</b> The document count to compare.<br/>
        <b>ReturnTrueIfMore:</b> Controls if the action returns true or false based on the comparison.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Counts all of the document objects within the batch and returns true or false based
        on the provided count.<br/><br/>

        This action only counts document nodes attached to the batch level object.
        Typically documents only exist off of the batch level.  If an application should have
        a document that is the child of another document node, the child document nodes
        are not included in the total count.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsDocumentCountMoreThan("1", "true")</b><br/>
          This example returns true if there are more than 1 document object in the batch.<br/><br/>

          <b>IsDocumentCountMoreThan("1", "false")</b><br/>
          This example returns false if there are more than 1 document object in the batch.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        if returnTrueIfMore is <b>true,</b> the action returns true if the document count exceeds the parameter, otherwise <b>false</b> is returned.<br/><br/>
        if returnTrueIfMore is <b>false,</b> the action returns false if the document count exceeds the parameter, otherwise <b>true</b> is returned.<br/>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding document objects.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    <method name="IsFirstDocumentInBatch" qi=": Checks to see if this is the first document in the batch.">
    <ap>
      None.<br/>
    </ap>
    <h>
      Checks to see if this is on an object in the first document in a batch.  If called on
      a document node, the action will return true if it is the first document in the batch.
      If called at a page or field level, the action will check to see if the parent
      document node is the first node in the batch.<br/><br/>
      <e>
        <b>IsFirstDocumentInBatch()</b><br/>
        If the current node belongs to the first document, the action will return true
        and the next action in the function will run.  If not, the action will return false
        and control will switch to the next function in the ruleset.
      </e>
    </h>
    <lvl>
      Document, Page or Field.<br/>
    </lvl>
    <ret>
      <b>True</b> if on the first document, or the current object in the first document.  Otherwise <b>False.</b>
    </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding document objects.
      </see>
    </method>
      
    <method name="IsFirstPageInBatch" qi=": Checks to see if this page is the first in the batch.">
      <ap>
        None.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Checks to see if this page is the first in the batch.  
        If documents have been created,
        it does not matter if this has been arranged as the first page object or if other
        page objects have been placed ahead of it in the DCO tree.  If the page was
        the first page created in the batch, it will return true.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>IsFirstPageInBatch()</b><br/>
          If the current page node is the first page in the batch, the action will return true
          and the next action in the function will run.  If not, the action will return false
          and control will switch to the next function in the ruleset.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        Page Level Only.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if it is the first page.  Otherwise <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding page objects.
      </see>
    </method>

      <method name="IsMultiPageDocument" qi=": Checks if the current document object has multiple pages."> 
          <ap>
              None.
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action returns true if the current document object contains multiple child pages.
              If called on a page or field object, the action will check the parent document object.<br/>
              <e>
                  <b>IsMultiPageDocument()</b><br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Document, page or field level.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False,</b> if called at the Batch level, the document object cannot be found,
              or if the document does not have more than 1 child.
              Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>

      <method name="IsSinglePageDocument" qi=": Checks if the current document contains a single page.">
          <ap>
              None.
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action is used to determine if the current object is a document with only 1 child page attached.
              <e>
                  <b>IsSinglePageDocument()</b><br/>
                  PopulateZNField()<br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>Document, page or field level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False,</b> if called at the Batch level, the document object cannot be found,
              or if the document has more than 1 child.
              <b>True,</b> if the document contains one page.
          </ret>
       </method>   
      

    <method name="JoinDocumentWithPreviousDocument" qi=": Combines the previous document into the current document.">
      <p name="Type"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Type:</b> Document Type of previous document (to be joined with the current document).<br/>
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Called on a document level object, the pages from the previous document
        are moved into this document.  The pages from the previous document will be placed
        in front of the pages in the current document.<br/><br/>
        
        The previous document object will be removed so the result is that the
        structure of both documents are merged into a single document level object.<br/><br/>
        
        If the type of the previous document does not match the type
        provided in the parameter, then the documents will not be joined.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>JoinDocumentToPreviousDocument("SeparatorDoc")</b><br/>
          The current document object will be merged with the previous document object,
          if the previous document type is "SeparatorDoc".<br/><br/>
          
          If the runtime DCO was like this before:<br/><br/>
          
          SeparatorDoc<br/>
          - Page1<br/>
          - Page2<br/>
          InvoiceDoc<br/>
          - Main_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/><br/>
          
          After calling the join action, the structure will look like this:<br/><br/>

          InvoiceDoc<br/>
          - Page1<br/>
          - Page2<br/>          
          - Main_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        Document Level Only.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding document objects.
      </see>
    </method>
    

    <method name="MoveBatchPageToPreviousDocument" qi=": Moves the current page without a document structure into a document object.">
      <p name="NewDocumentType"/>
      <ap>
        <b>NewDocumentType:</b> If the current page is the batch's first child object, a new document object
        will be created with the type specified.<br/>
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        The current page will be moved to become a child of the first document node
        that is previous to the current page node.  If the page is the first object
        in the batch, then a new document node will be created and this page object
        will be moved to the new document, becoming a child page of the new document object.<br/><br/>
        
        This action must be run on a page object that is directly attached to the batch level object.
        The "Other" page type is an example of a page that does not have a document hierarchy and
        is directly attached to the batch DCO node.<br/><br/>        
        
        If this action is run on a page node that is already in a document structure,
        the action will exit without moving the page.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>MoveBatchPageToPreviousDocument("OtherDoc")</b><br/>
          This example must be run on a page object that is directly attached to the
          batch level object. This is a page that has not yet been assigned to a document
          structure.  The page will be moved into the previous sibling document, if it
          is a document object.  If there isn't a previous document object, then
          a new document object will be created and assigned the provided type.<br/><br/>
          
          With the DCO structure like this:<br/><br/>
          
          Batch<br/>
          - Document1<br/>
          - InvoicePage<br/>
          - Document2<br/>
          
          If the action is called on "InvoicePage", then the page will be moved
          to the "Document1" object.<br/><br/>

          Batch<br/>
          - InvoicePage<br/>
          - Document1<br/>          
          - Document2<br/>

          If the action is called on "InvoicePage", then a new document node
          of type "OtherDoc" will be created and "InvoicePage"
          will be moved to the new "OtherDoc" object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        Page Level, but will execute only if the page's parent is the batch level object.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b><br/>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding page objects.
      </see>
    </method>


      <method name="PageIDBySequenceTypes" qi=": Assigns a type to all pages of type 'Other' from a list of types.">
          <p name="SequenceTypes" qi="A comma separated list of page types to assign."/>
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action processes all pages with a type of "Other", assigning them page types
              as specified by the input parameter list. The action will process each page and assign each type
              in order as they appear in the DCO tree.
              When the end of the list is reached, assignments will start again from the start of the list.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  <b>PageIDBySequenceTypes("Front,Back")</b><br/><br/>
                  
                  The action will set the page types of the batch to Front, Back, Front, Back, Front, and so on.
                  It is assumed that the pages have all been ingested in the correct order, or that earlier
                  actions have determined the correct order and have either properly ordered the pages
                  or have created a correct list used as a parameter.<br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Batch level.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>
      

      <method name="PageIDByVariableChange" qi=": Assigns page types using a main and trailing page paradigm based on the value of a DCO variable.">
          <p name="VariableToWatch" qi="The name of the variable to check in each page object."/>
          <p name="MainPageType" qi="The type to assign to the first page of the document."/>
          <p name="TrailingPageType" qi="The type to assign to all trailing pages in the document."/>
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              This action processes all pages within a batch and sets the page type according to the input parameters.
              When pages are ready for PageID, a variable can be "watched" so that when it changes
              a new page type will be triggered.<br/><br/>
              
              One use case for this action is to create the document structure from
              multi-page TIFFs or PDFs that went through a VScan process and have
              been split into single images.<br/><br/>

              The action will watch the DCO variable that is identified by the first parameter.  The first page
              in the batch will be set to the page type identified by the second parameter.  The action will
              then continue to look at the DCO variable for each subsequent page.  If the value of the variable
              does not change, each subsequent page will considered a trailing page and is assigned the page type identified by the third
              parameter.  Processing all of the pages in the batch, this continues until the value of the DCO variable changes.  When a change is
              detected, it indicates a new main page and that page type is set to the page type identified in the second parameter.
              Subsequent pages are then set to the page type identified by the third parameter, until reaching
              the last page of the batch, or the watched variable changes again.<br/><br/>

              While this action is attached to the batch level node,
              the DCO variable identified in the first parameter is a variable that must exist on
              every page level DCO object in the batch.<br/><br/>
              <e>
                  <b>PageIDByVariableChange("ScanSrcPath", "Main_Page", "Trailing_Page")</b><br/><br/>

                  In this example, the variable ScanSrcPath contains the path to the
                  source image. When TIF pages are split from a multiple page TIF or PDF,
                  the ScanSrcPath variable identifies the parent document that was split into pages.
                  This variable will be the same for all pages that came from the parent and will change
                  on pages that came from a different parent.
                  This action will watch the contents of the variable ScanSrcPath on each page within
                  the batch. When the variable changes,
                  the first page will be set to the page type Main_Page, and any
                  other pages that continue to have the same value in ScanSrcPath that immediately follow will
                  have the page type set to Trailing_Page.
                  The action will scan through each page, looking at the value of ScanSrcPath.  When it changes again,
                  the first page will be set to the page type Main_Page and any subsequent pages with the same
                  value in ScanSrcPage will have the page type set to Trailing_Page.  This will continue until
                  all pages in the batch have been set.  It is also assumed that after splitting a document into
                  multiple pages, the parent document has been deleted from the DCO tree prior to calling this action.
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Batch level.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>


      <method name="PopulateTaxType" qi=": Loads the search terms for the Taxes field.">
          <p name="Types"/>
          <ap>
              A comma separated list of types to process for creation of the tax lineitems.<br/>
          </ap>
          <h>
              For each tax type provided, a "Tax_Type" and "Tax_Value" lineitem field is created.
              The current field must not contain children.<br/><br/>

              This action is for APT and not intended for general use.<br/>
              <e>
                  <b>PopulateTaxType("Sales,VAT,GST")</b>
                  <br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Field Level.  The Taxes/TaxLineitem/Tax_Value field in APT.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>TRUE.</b>
          </ret>
      </method>
      

    <method name="PromotePageToDocument" qi=": Converts a page object to a document object.">
      <p name="NewDocumentType"/>
      <p name="NewDocumentID"/>
      <ap>
        <b>NewDocumentType:</b> The new document type for this page node.<br/><br/>

        <b>NewDocumentID:</b> The new document ID for this page node. If a parameter is provided
        this ID must be unique within the batch. 
        Leave this field blank to have the action create a unique ID.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action converts the current page object to a document level object.  This action is useful for situations
        where file has been ingested as a page level node and it is desired to change the page node to a document node,
        so the file associated with the node is now a document level object.<br/><br/>

        This is achieved by creating a new document node of the type specified in the parameter.
        The variables from the current page node, such as IMAGEFILE and other variables will be copied
        over to the new document node.  If the page has associated data files, they will now be
        associated with the current document using the same variable names.<br/><br/>

        The ID of the new document object will be unique, it will not duplicate the ID from the original page object.  
        Once the new document object is created, the existing page DCO node will have its status changed
        to "75", indicating that it is in "Deleted" state. This action does not delete the page from the object hierarchy.
        To fully remove the page object from the batch, use an action like DeleteDCOChildWithStatus("75") to remove
        pages that have been marked for deletion.<br/><br/>

        The current page node must be a direct child of the batch.  The new document node will be a child of the batch.<br/><br/>

        Any child fields of the current page will not be moved to the document object.  
        Any child fields and associated data will be deleted when the page node is deleted.<br/><br/>

        After this action is called, it is recommended that no further rules are run on the page object as it will be removed. 
        Additionally, any rules which will be run on the new document object should be in a 
        following ruleset, not the same ruleset as this action.<br/><br/>        
        <e>
          Ruleset1<br/>
          - Rule1 (Attached to a page DCO)<br/>
          - - Function1<br/>
          - - - <b>PromotePageToDocument(“Invoice”,“”)</b><br/>
          - Rule2 (Attached to batch close)
          - - Function2<br/>
          - - - DeleteDCOChildWithStatus("75")<br/><br/>

          Ruleset2<br/>
          - Rule3 (Attached to a document level “Invoice” DCO)<br/>
          - - Function3<br/>
          - - - SetNamePattern(“2”)<br/>
          - - - rrSet(“1”,”@X.convPdfIgnoreContent”)<br/>
          - - - rrSet(“0”,”@X.y_createLayout”)<br/>
          - - - PDFFREDocumentToImage(“300”,”18”,”33”,”32”,”.bw.tif”,”col.tif”,”gray.tif”,”0”,”False”,100”)<br/><br/>

          This example assumes that Rule1 is attached to a page level object that needs to be promoted to a document 
          object, Rule2 is attached to the batch close event and 
          Rule3 is attached to an “Invoice” document object.  The action PromotePageToDocument 
          will convert the current page object to a new document object of type “Invoice”.  The original
          page is marked with status 75 to indicate it is deleted.<br/><br/>

          Rule2 runs at the batch level and deletes all children that have a status of 75. This action
          must be the last action in a ruleset.  Any subsequent actions must be in a separate ruleset.
          If desired, DeleteDCOChildWithStatus could be in its own ruleset as long as the ruleset
          does not contain additional actions.  Any subsequent actions must be in a new ruleset.<br/><br/>
          
          Ruleset1 will complete and then the next ruleset2 will run on the new document node, 
          converting the associated PDF file into multiple TIF images.  
          A new page object will be created for each TIF and each page will be a child of the Invoice document.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page level only.  The page must be a direct child of the batch object.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Returns <b>false</b> if the NewDocumentType parameter is blank or if an error occurs, otherwise <b>true</b>.
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding field objects.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    
    <method name="RefreshFields" qi=": Updates the Data file for a page in a batch with new fields and also preserves the data for previously created fields.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Updates the runtime field data file for a page 
        with new fields and also preserves the data for previously created fields. If additional fields have been
        created after a previous call to CreateFields, this action will create the required
        child DCO field objects for the current page for the newly created fields.<br/><br/>

        This action is similar to CreateFields except that if there is already a Data file for a page then it will not
        erase the previously created fields and will just add new fields to the page.
        Existing fields will remain unchanged.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          SetPageType("Main_Page")<br/>
          <b>RefreshFields()</b><br/><br/>
          
          This sequence of actions assigns the page type "Main_Page" to the current
          page, then will initialize any new fields that belong to "Main_Page"
          that do not already exist on the current page.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if successful. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding field objects.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="RemoveDocumentStructure" qi=": Removes the document hierarchy from the batch.">
      <ap>
        None.
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action will flatten the document - page hierarchy from the batch.  If the batch consists of multiple documents,
        each with a set of pages, the document level is removed and all of the pages become a flat structure.
        Once complete, there is no distinction of sets of pages within a document.<br/><br/>
        
        It is recommended that this action is called as the last action within a ruleset.
        Place any following actions into a follow on ruleset.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>RemoveDocumentStructure()</b><br/><br/>
          
          If a batch has a document structure like this:<br/><br/>

          Batch<br/>
          - Invoice<br/>
          - - Main_Page<br/>
          - - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Invoice<br/>
          - - Main_Page<br/>
          - - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Invoice<br/>
          - - Main_Page<br/>
          - - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - - Trailing_Page<br/><br/>

          After this action completes, the DCO structure will be:<br/><br/>

          Batch<br/>
          - Main_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Main_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Main_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/>
          - Trailing_Page<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for more information regarding the DCO hierarchy.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SetDCOStatus" qi=": Assigns a value to the Status property of the current object of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <p name="Type" type="string" />
      <ap>
        An Integer representing the new status.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Assigns a value to the Status property of the current object of the Document Hierarchy.
        The status indicates various states of the object. It may indicate the last step
        that was performed on the object or it can convey that the object is in an error
        state.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          CheckDCOType("Invoice")<br/>
          <b>SetDCOStatus("1")</b><br/>
          This sequence checks to see if the current object of the Document Hierarchy is a 
          an Invoice.  If so, the value of the object's Status property is set to "1".<br/><br/>
          
          Function1 Test Date Range<br/>
          - IsFieldDateEqualOrBefore("@DATE()")<br/>
          - IsFieldDateWithinXDays("7")<br/>
          - <b>SetDCOStatus("0")</b><br/>
          Function2 Date Outside Range<br/>
          - <b>SetDCOStatus("1")</b><br/>
          Assuming this is called on a field object node, these actions first validate that the
          current field contains a valid date that is equal to or before the current date.  
          The next action then confirms that the date is within 7 days of today's date.
          If both of these tests pass the status of the field is set to "0"
          indicating that it does not have any errors.
          If either "IsField..." action fails, then control passes
          to the following function which sets the object status to "1"
          which indicates it has a failure.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the input parameter is not an integer, otherwise <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetDCOType, SetPageType<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help of this action library for information regarding DCO types and status.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SetDCOType" qi=": Assigns a value to the Type property of the current object of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <p name="Type" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Type:</b> The new value to assign to the current DCO node's Type property.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Assigns a value to the Type property of the current object of the Document Hierarchy.
        When changing the type of the current node, the status value of the node
        is left unchanged.<br/><br/>
        
        This action can set the type of a page similar to the action SetPageType.  SetPageType
        also has some slightly different features refer to that action for details.<br/>        
                
        The batch type defaults to the name of the application. While this action allows the
        batch type to be changed, it is not recommended as it could cause unintended results.<br/><br/>
        
        This action allows field types to be changed. Typically the field type and field ID are
        the same value.<br/><br/>
        
        An object's type value may not contain a double quote.  If a double quote is provided,
        it will be changed to a single quote.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          CheckLastDCOType("Separator")<br/>
          <b>SetDCOType("Invoice")</b><br/><br/>

          This sequence checks to see if the previous sibling object of the application's
          document hierarchy was a Separator page. If so, it
          sets Invoice as the Type property of the current object.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetDCOStatus, SetPageStatus, SetDocumentType<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help of this action library for information regarding DCO types and status.
    </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SetDocumentStatus" qi=": The action assigns a status to the current document node or parent document node.">
      <p name="Type" type="string" />
    <ap>
      <b>Type:</b> An integer representing the new status.<br/><br/>    
    </ap>
    <h>
        Similar to the SetDCOStatus action, but works at the Document, Page or Field level.  If called at the
        page or field level, the status is set for the parent document object and the status of the current
        child object will not be changed.<br/><br/>
      <e>
          CheckDCOType("Invoice")<br/>
          <b>SetDCOStatus("1")</b><br/>
          This sequence checks to see if the current object of the Document Hierarchy is a 
          an Invoice.  If so, the value of the object's Status property is set to "1".<br/><br/>
          
          Function1 Test Date Range<br/>
          - IsFieldDateEqualOrBefore("@DATE()")<br/>
          - IsFieldDateWithinXDays("7")<br/>
          - <b>SetDCOStatus("0")</b><br/>
          Function2 Date Outside Range<br/>
          - <b>SetDCOStatus("1")</b><br/>
          Assuming this is called on a field object node, these actions first validate that the
          current field contains a valid date that is equal to or before the current date.  
          The next action then confirms that the date is within 7 days of today's date.
          If both of these tests pass the status of the field is set to "0"
          indicating that it does not have any errors.
          If either "IsField..." action fails, then control passes
          to the following function which sets the object status to "1"
          which indicates it has a failure.<br/><br/>
      </e>
    </h>  
    <lvl>Document, Page or field level.</lvl>
    <ret>
      <b>False</b> if the input parameter is not an integer, otherwise <b>True.</b>
    </ret>
     <see>
     SetPageType, SetDCOStatus, SetDCOType, SetDCOStatus<br/>
       Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding an object's status.
    </see>
  </method>
    
     <method name="SetDocumentType" qi=": The action assigns a type to the current document or parent document object of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <p name="Type" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Type:</b> The new value to assign to the Document DCO node's Type property.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Similar to the SetDCOType action, but works at the Document, Page or Field level to set the
        document object type.  If called at the page or
        field level, the type is still set for the parent document object and the type of the current
        object will not be changed.<br/><br/>

        When changing the type of a page node, the status value of the node
        is left unchanged.<br/><br/>

        An object's type value may not contain a double quote.  If a double quote is provided,
        it will be changed to a single quote.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          The application's scan task typically assigns Other as the Page Type and 49 as the default
          Page Status for every successfully scanned image in the batch.<br/><br/>

          The sequence below is an example of a rule that changes the type of the current page to Invoice and assigns a page new status:<br/>
          <b>SetPageType("Invoice")</b><br/>
          SetPageStatus("1")<br/><br/>

          This combination sets the page as an Invoices page, and gives it a status of 1. 
          This means that the page has not yet been validated and must be processed by a 
          task which applies Validation rules.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page and Field levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the current DCO node is not a page or a field or if the provided field does not have a parent object.
        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetPageStatus, SetDCOStatus, SetDCOType<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help of this action library for information regarding DCO types and status.
      </see>
    </method>
      
    <method name="SetFieldConfidence" qi=": Sets the confidence for all characters in a field to the same value.">
      <p name="Target" type="string" />
      <p name="NewConfidence" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Target:</b> A smart parameter that resolves to a DCO field node.<br/><br/>
        <b>NewConfidence:</b> The confidence value (1-10) to be assigned to the field's characters.
        If the parameter is invalid or out of range, it will default to 10, which is the
        highest confidence.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
     </ap>
      <h>
        This action unconditionally sets the confidence values for every character within a field to a specific value.
        This action can help change confidence levels when the preceding actions are all successful
        and it is desired to update the field confidence automatically to show that all characters are valid.
        Conversely, it is possible to set all of the characters to a low confidence value to ensure
        that the characters are all reviewed in a follow on process or in a verification panel.<br/><br/>
        
        If a field has text such as "Hello Datacap" and the characters were recognized
        in order with these confidences, "8,8,8,8,4,4,8,8,8,3,9,9", calling this
        action with a confidence value of 8 will set the confidence for each character
        in the field to a value of 8, regardless of its current value.<br/><br/>        
        
        For example, if a set of calculations involving one or more fields is
        successful, this may indicate that the confidence level of those fields' values should be forced
        to a high confidence - regardless of the
        confidence set by the recognition engine.  After the success of the calculation, the rules
        can call <b>SetFieldConfidence("@P\MyField","10")</b> to unconditionally re-set 
        the confidence values of the fields to the highest possible confidence.
        The examples below show how the ValidationsAndTextAdjustments action
        CalculateFields could be used to automatically validate the results
        of field recognition. Applications can have other kinds of use cases that
        would allow a field to be automatically validated and set to a high
        confidence without requiring operator interaction.  Another example
        could be a database look-up of a recognized name, or other automatic type
        of confirmation that one or more fields are believed to be recognized
        correctly even if the recognition engine is unsure of some of the characters.<br/><br/>               
        
        When viewing the confidence directly in the field level XML, values are actually
        0 - 9 to permit one character to reflect the value of each character within
        the xml.  This illustrates that to the user the range is exposed as 1 - 10 
        but internally it is sometimes treated as 0-9. When developing and debugging
        an application, be aware of this potential situation.<br/><br/>
      <e>                
        <b>SetFieldConfidence("@P\GrossSalary", "10")</b><br/>
        This example sets all characters in the field "GrossSalary" to a confidence of 10.
        The action could be called on a page level DCO node or on a sibling field level
        DCO node and the smart parameter would resolve correctly as first it would
        resolve to the current page and then to the child DCO node called "GrossSalary".<br/><br/>
                
        <b>SetFieldConfidence("@P\AdjustedPay" , "1")</b><br/>
        Similar to the previous example, this one will lower the confidence of each
        character in the field "AdjustedPay" to a low confidence of 1.
        If called on a page level node, it will navigate from the current page to find
        the child "AdjustedPay".  If called on a field level node, it will navigate up
        to the parent page, then look for the sibling field "AdjustedPay".<br/><br/>
        
        <b>SetFieldConfidence("@X", "1")</b><br/>
        Similar to the previous action, however this one would be expected to
        be called directly on a field level DCO node and the current field's confidence
        would be adjusted.<br/><br/><br/>

        
        This next example shows using SetFieldConfidence in the context
        of a test run before setting the confidence.<br/><br/>
        
        CalculateFields("@STRING('1TotalWages' + '2TaxableInterest' + "3Unemployment' = '4AdjustedGross')")<br/><br/>              
        <b>SetFldConfidence("@P\1totalWages", "10")</b><br/>
        <b>SetFldConfidence("@P\2TaxableInterest", "10")</b><br/>
        <b>SetFldConfidence("@P\3Unemployment", "10")</b><br/>
        <b>SetFldConfidence("@P\4AdjustedGross", "10")</b><br/>
        If the calculation works, the application can assume that all of the characters are read
        correctly because all of the fields summed up to the correct value 
        and <b>SetFieldConfidence</b> can adjust the fields to high confidence.
        Note, in this example it would be a good practice to also add a check
        that the values are all non-zero to eliminate a completely "bad read".<br/><br/>
      </e>
    </h>  
    <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
    <ret>
      Always <b>True.</b> If the target input parameter is invalid, no confidence levels 
      are changed and a message is logged.
    </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding a field's confidence.
      </see>
  </method>

    
    <method name="SetObjectID" qi=": Renames the target dco node with a new object ID.">
      <p name="NewID" type="string" />
      <p name="TargetObject" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>NewID:</b> The value that will be used as the new Target ID. If this parameter is blank, the
        text property of the current object will be used.<br/><br/>

        <b>TargetObject:</b> A smart parameter referencing a target which is receiving the new ID.
        If this parameter is blank, then the current object will be the target.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Allows an object's ID to be changed at runtime. An object's ID should be unique.<br/><br/>

        An object's ID is set by Datacap actions and clients when new objects are created.
        The assigned ID usually does not need to be manually set or changed. Changing an object's
        ID can change how rules are run. An object's ID should not be manually changed unless it
        is absolutely necessary as it can produce unintended results. If this action must
        be used, it is recommended that this be run in the last rule of a
        ruleset so any subsequent rules are run in a new ruleset.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetObjectID("@F.MySourceVar","@P")</b><br/><br/>

          Assuming this action is called on a field object, new ID value is obtained from
          the variable called "MySourceVar".  The value is then assigned to
          of the calling object's Parent page object.<br/><br/>

          <b>SetObjectID("","")</b><br/><br/>

          Assuming this action is called on a field object, new ID value is obtained from
          the text value of the current field.  The value is then assigned to the ID
          of the current field.<br/><br/>
        </e>
        <br/>
        <br/>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the action cannot locate the target object.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help of this action library for information regarding DCO types and status.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetPageStatus" qi=": The action assigns a page status to the current page or the parent page object of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <p name="Type" type="string" />
    <ap>
      <b>Type:</b> An integer representing the new status.<br/><br/>    
    </ap>
    <h>
        Similar to the SetDCOStatus action, but works at the Page or Field level.  If called at the
        field level, the status is set for the parent page object and the status of the field
        object will not be changed.<br/><br/>
      <e>
          CheckDCOType("Invoice")<br/>
          <b>SetDCOStatus("1")</b><br/>
          This sequence checks to see if the current object of the Document Hierarchy is a 
          an Invoice.  If so, the value of the object's Status property is set to "1".<br/><br/>
          
          Function1 Test Date Range<br/>
          - IsFieldDateEqualOrBefore("@DATE()")<br/>
          - IsFieldDateWithinXDays("7")<br/>
          - <b>SetDCOStatus("0")</b><br/>
          Function2 Date Outside Range<br/>
          - <b>SetDCOStatus("1")</b><br/>
          Assuming this is called on a field object node, these actions first validate that the
          current field contains a valid date that is equal to or before the current date.  
          The next action then confirms that the date is within 7 days of today's date.
          If both of these tests pass the status of the field is set to "0"
          indicating that it does not have any errors.
          If either "IsField..." action fails, then control passes
          to the following function which sets the object status to "1"
          which indicates it has a failure.<br/><br/>
      </e>
    </h>  
    <lvl>Page or field level.</lvl>
    <ret>
      <b>False</b> if the input parameter is not an integer, otherwise <b>True.</b>
    </ret>
     <see>
     SetPageType, SetDCOStatus, SetDCOType, SetDCOStatus<br/>
       Refer to the top level help topic for more information regarding an object's status.
    </see>
  </method>
      

    <method name="SetPageType" qi=": Assigns a Page Type to the current Page or parent Page object of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <p name="Type" type="string" />
      <ap>
        <b>Type:</b> The new value to assign to the current DCO node's Type property.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Similar to the SetDCOType action, but works at the Page or Field level.  If called at the
        field level, the type is still set for the parent page object and the type of the field
        object will not be changed.<br/><br/>

        When changing the type of a page node, the status value of the node
        is left unchanged.<br/><br/>

        An object's type value may not contain a double quote.  If a double quote is provided,
        it will be changed to a single quote.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          The application's scan task typically assigns Other as the Page Type and 49 as the default
          Page Status for every successfully scanned image in the batch.<br/><br/>

          The sequence below is an example of a rule that changes the type of the current page to Invoice and assigns a page new status:<br/>
          <b>SetPageType("Invoice")</b><br/>
          SetPageStatus("1")<br/><br/>

          This combination sets the page as an Invoices page, and gives it a status of 1. 
          This means that the page has not yet been validated and must be processed by a 
          task which applies Validation rules.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page and Field levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the current DCO node is not a page or a field or if the provided field does not have a parent object.
        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetPageStatus, SetDCOStatus, SetDCOType<br/><br/>
        Refer to the top level help of this action library for information regarding DCO types and status.
      </see>
    </method>
    
  </com>
</rrx>
